---
// A short list of links to other pages, the reference's See also and a language reference
// entry's examples: one column on a phone, two from 40rem, run down the columns in order.
// The caller writes the <li> items; this draws the list around them.
---

<ul class="link-columns"><slot /></ul>

<style>
  /* No markers, the way the footer's two columns of links have none: a marker hangs in the
     gutter to the left of its item, so the second column's markers sat in the gap beside the
     first column's text. The space between two items sits under each item, not over it: the
     top of a flowed column drops the space over an item, which left the first column's
     first item 8px below the second column's. In one column it is a margin, so the last
     one folds into the gap before the next section. In two it is padding, which a column
     break keeps whole, so the list is as tall as its longer column at any count.
     These outrank `.doc ul` and `.doc li`. */
  .link-columns { padding-inline-start: 0; list-style: none }
  .link-columns :global(li) { margin-block: 0 8px; padding-inline-start: 0; break-inside: avoid }
  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .link-columns { columns: 2; column-gap: 32px }
    .link-columns :global(li) { margin-block-end: 0; padding-block-end: 8px }
  }
</style>
